\name{LatticeLineLayerProcessor}
\alias{LatticeLineLayerProcessor}
\title{lattice Line Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads the lines an \code{xyplot()} panel draws -- \code{type = "l"}, \code{"b"}, \code{"o"},
the average line of \code{"a"} -- and a \code{qqmath()} panel's, as a \code{line} layer
with one series per group; and the staircase of \code{type = "s"} or \code{"S"} as
a \code{step} layer.

A line is read in the order it was drawn, which for lattice is the order
of the data: \code{panel.xyplot()} joins the points as they come, so unsorted
x draws a zigzag, and reading it sorted would describe a line the chart
does not show. A missing y breaks the drawn line and is emitted as a gap
(\code{y: null}) at its x. A row with no x breaks it too, but has no position
a gap could be put at -- the frontend places a line's points by their x,
a number or a string -- and is left out, as py-maidr leaves out the
matplotlib points with no x: the pieces on either side are read as one.

A staircase is drawn through \verb{2n - 1} vertices for \code{n} samples, sorted
by x; the samples are the odd vertices, and \code{stepDirection} says which
way each riser runs: \code{"hv"} for \code{"s"}, \code{"vh"} for \code{"S"}. A missing value
breaks the staircase into pieces, and maidr.js 4.11.0 outlines most
samples of a staircase drawn in pieces at a corner rather than at the
sample; what is read is right, only the outline is not. With
\code{distribute.type = TRUE} that is the type of the layer's own groups,
which the adapter keeps apart by direction, not of the chart's \code{type}
vector as a whole.

A line on a factor axis runs through the levels' positions: its x is the
level's name, and on a factor y axis the name goes with the position as
\code{label}, since the frontend sonifies y and needs it a number. A date or
date-time axis is emitted in milliseconds with a date format, as the
frontend reads a time (\code{lattice_time_milliseconds()}).
}
